7 Moreouer, thoſe things which happen vnto vs are either proſperous, or hurtfull;
and as well hurtfull things, as proſperous, be either great or ſmall. The ſmall things as well in the kind of hurtfull, as of happie, dooth
Ariſtotle take, and ſaith; that thoſe are of no moment, either to change the condition of life, or to driue it from the former eſtate. Which without doubt we are to vnderſtand, as touching the bleſſed, and them that be indued with vertue: otherwiſe, the common ſort of men, and they which be of no account, are eaſilie and in a maner for the ſmaleſt cauſes of all, troubled and diſquieted, which good and graue men will not in like ſort doo. Then he taketh the other member;
But admit they be great things which doo happen; thoſe, if they be proſperous, will without doubt increaſe the bleſſednes of life: bicauſe they be made for adorning of the ſame. Felicitie beyond his owne proper bounds cannot be increaſed, ſéeing it is the chéefeſt good of all. But in the bleſſednes of his owne kind it may be greater, & it may be leſſe. But ſince that Ariſtotle ſaith, that bleſſednes may be adorned, it ſéemeth the ſame may be compared with a ſingular forme or beautie; which though by it ſelfe it be delightfull, yet is it more ſwéete, if there be anie ornament decentlie ioined therevnto. And the art of handicraft, when it obteineth inſtruments more fit, dooth expreſſe a greater cunning, than when groſſer tooles are applied therevnto. But if on the contrarie part, the miſfortunes which befall are gréeuous and manie, I ſaie that they preſſe downe, and blemiſh felicitie.
